import "pkg:/source/logging/Logger.bs"
import "pkg:/source/utils/misc.bs"

sub init()
  m.log = new log.Logger("AuthManager")
end sub

' canActivate guard: see AuthManager.xml header. Invoked by the router on the render thread
' during navigateTo, once per guarded route. A cheap SYNCHRONOUS token check only, no network
' (launch-time AboutMe re-validation stays in the login flow).
'
' Returns true when a token is present (allow the navigation), else false (refuse it).
'
' A refused request is dropped, not redirected and not kept for after sign-in. Nothing a
' person can do while signed out requests a guarded route: the session ends only through
' user.Logout(), and every caller then routes to a pre-login screen. So a request that reaches
' here is a stray (a late remote-control command, for one), and the viewer is already on a
' sign-in screen. Moving them would take them off it: a redirect to /login skipped the user
' picker, where Quick Connect is, and landed on a sign-in screen even with no server saved.
' The router rejects the navigation and JRScene.failNavigation() puts focus back on the
' current screen. A deep link that arrives while signed out is the intent that does survive
' sign-in, and it has its own stash (stashedDeepLink). Logged as a warning because it should
' not happen.
function canActivate(currentRequest as object) as dynamic
  if isValidAndNotEmpty(m.global.user.authToken) then return true

  path = ""
  if isValid(currentRequest) and isValid(currentRequest.path) then path = currentRequest.path
  m.log.warn("guarded route requested while signed out; ignoring it", path)
  return false
end function
